{
 "about": "The season series, from an MLB summary. season_series.py (tests/unit/test_season_series.py) and the iPhone's Models/SeasonSeries.swift (SeasonSeriesGridTests.swift) must both give these answers. The record is counted from the finished games' winner flags, as of `this_game` (the summary's own game, header.id) when it is one of the series' games: games after it don't count. A game is finished when statusType.completed is true; status post counts only with no statusType. A series is won when all its games are played, or a playoff series when one team has more than half of totalCompetitions. Order: playoff, season, current, preseason, then the rest as sent; a series with the same games as one before it is left out. `names` is team id to the name shown; a team not in it falls back to the game's displayName. A case reads a real summary from tests/fixtures/<fixture> or the summary written out here.",
 "cases": [
  {
   "name": "regular season game 12 of 13: as of this game, as ESPN says, though the Nationals took game 13",
   "fixture": "espn/summary_final_MLB_401817091.json",
   "this_game": "401817091",
   "names": {"20": "Nationals", "21": "Mets"},
   "expected": [
    {"label": "Regular Season Series", "text": "Mets lead 7-5", "games": 13},
    {"label": "Current Series", "text": "Tied 1-1", "games": 3},
    {"label": "Preseason Series", "text": "Nationals won 4-1", "games": 5}
   ],
   "expected_line": "Regular Season Series: Mets lead 7-5"
  },
  {
   "name": "World Series game 4: Brewers lead 3-1, not the 4-1 they won it by; the current series is the playoff's games and is left out",
